A page can be legible to a human and opaque to a program. Two strings that look identical may be a headword and an example of its use; two overlapping strokes may represent a staff line and a notehead that must move together. A language for publishing has to decide when to preserve those identities and when to replace them by geometry or by marks on a target device. As with the blazon language in Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-186}, a rendering is not a substitute for the structured description.

\section{Keep Text Structure Available to Downstream Tools}

J\"orgen Pind's \emph{Computers, Typesetting, and Lexicography} describes using \TeX{} for dictionary production and argues for logical encoding while questioning whether tags can be wholly neutral with respect to typographic needs. A dictionary editor can encode a pronunciation as a pronunciation and let a style choose the font. But if an entry includes a transcribed specimen whose original lineation matters, those breaks must survive as source information. The distinction is not between important and unimportant whitespace; it is between evidence being preserved and layout being chosen for an output.

Prefer a typed tree containing entries, senses, examples, and references over concatenating strings of final markup. In the tree, interpolating a title can produce text without allowing bracket characters in that text to open new document nodes. Will Crichton and Shriram Krishnamurthi's \emph{A Core Calculus for Documents: Or, Lambda: The Ultimate Document} separates string and tree domains in its analysis of document constructors and develops explicit treatments of references and reactivity. Both choices affect when layout must be recomputed and what a reference can name.

\section{Worked Example: A Constraint-Based Annotation}

Consider attaching a reading label to a score fragment in a dictionary entry. Give the fragment a horizontal span from \(0\) to \(80\) points and a named anchor at \(x=50\). A vertical leader should start at a notation mark at \((50,8)\) and end four points below the label's baseline. Set the baseline \(y=22\) in an upward coordinate system. The solver places the leader's end at \((50,18)\); the renderer centers the label at \((50,22)\) using the label's measured advance. An advance of \(24\) gives horizontal extents \(38\) and \(62\) for the label's advance box. That box is not necessarily the outline's bounding box: an italic glyph can overhang it.

This separation of placement equations and drawing actions is inspired by Christopher J. Van Wyk's \emph{A Graphics Typesetting Language}, which describes IDEAL boxes with constraints on significant points and actions at those points. Omitting the baseline condition leaves a degree of freedom; adding a second incompatible height condition has no solution. Report those problems differently and identify the responsible constraints before emitting a drawing. Ulrik Vieth's \emph{Understanding the Aesthetics of Math Typesetting} supplies a parallel example from \TeX: the algorithm for assembling a formula is only part of the result, because font metrics determine its concrete shifts and adjustments.

\section{Lower to Marks Without Losing the Source of Truth}

In \emph{Music Printing in Renaissance Venice: The Scotto Press (1539--1572)}, Jane A. Bernstein contrasts multiple-impression printing, which required alignment of separately printed material, with single-impression type combining a musical symbol and a piece of staff. The change solved an alignment problem at the cost of visible joins between pieces of staff. That is a physical example of why a publishing backend should not dictate the source model. Encode the relationship between the note and staff; let each target choose how to produce the marks.

Test the annotation with a different font and a translated score origin. The named anchor must still follow the notation mark, the end gap must remain four points, and the label width may change. Also test that both screen and print variants preserve the entry's reference identifier even when they choose different page breaks. For reproducing a layout, record font metrics, renderer version, page dimensions, and style inputs. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/44-hermetic-compilation-capsules.md}{dossier's Hermetic Compilation Capsules chapter} offers the corresponding approach to capturing a build's dependencies. A document build whose fonts are selected from unrecorded system state does not have a reproducible layout contract.
